== Ray-based propagation
<app3.2>
Beam propagation is modeled in geometric optics via the eikonal (ray) system derived from Fermat's principle in an inhomogeneous medium: $ frac(d upright(bold(r)), d s) = upright(bold(v)) \, #h(2em) frac(d upright(bold(v)), d s) = frac(1, n #h(-1em) (upright(bold(r)))) thin nabla n #h(-1em) (upright(bold(r))) \, $<eq:eikonal> with the unit-speed constraint $lr(bar.v.double upright(bold(v)) bar.v.double) = 1$ enforced at every step. We launch $N_r = 500$ rays at the entrance plane $y = 0$, with initial lateral positions $x_0 in \[ - 25 \, 25 \] med mu upright(m)$ uniformly spaced (the central ray is omitted to avoid symmetry-locking artifacts), and initial direction $upright(bold(v))_0 = \( 0 \, 1 \)$. Numerical integration uses an explicit forward-Euler update with fixed arclength increment $d s = 0.5 med mu upright(m)$ for 1200 steps; after each update, $upright(bold(v))$ is renormalized to satisfy $lr(bar.v.double upright(bold(v)) bar.v.double) = 1$. Rays that exit the computational domain are terminated.

== Energy-conserving intensity reconstruction
<app3.3>
The spatiotemporal intensity is reconstructed by superposing the energy carried by each geometrical ray along its path. Each ray $j$ is assigned a launch weight $w_j$ proportional to a Gaussian in its initial lateral coordinate $x_(0 \, j)$, $ tilde(w)_j #h(0em) prop #h(0em) exp #h(-1em) (- frac(x_(0 \, j)^2, 2 sigma_w^2)) \, #h(2em) sigma_w = 10 med mu upright(m) \, $ and we normalize the weights so that $ w_j #h(0em) = #h(0em) frac(tilde(w)_j, sum_ell tilde(w)_ell) \, #h(2em) sum_j w_j = 1 . $ The energy attributed to ray $j$ is then $ E_j #h(0em) = #h(0em) w_j thin E_(upright(t r a n s)) . $ Along the discrete trajectory of ray $j$, indexed by the set of in-bounds arclength samples $k in cal(S)_j$ at positions $\( x_k \, y_k \)$, we distribute the ray energy uniformly (no absorption in this run). Writing $N_j = \| cal(S)_j \|$, the elemental energy per sample is $E_j \/ N_j$. At each sample we deposit this energy into the Eulerian $\( x \, y \)$ grid by a compact, normalized $5 times 5$ Gaussian kernel $K$ (unit sum) centered at the nearest grid node. Dividing by the voxel 4-volume $Delta x thin Delta y thin tau$ converts deposited energy to intensity. Concretely, the intensity increment at grid node $\( x_i \, y_m \)$ due to sample $\( j \, k \)$ is $ Delta I_(i \, m)^(\( j \, k \)) #h(0em) = #h(0em) frac(E_j \/ N_j, tau thin Delta x thin Delta y) #h(0em) K #h(-1em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] x_i - x_k \, #h(0em) y_m - y_k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $ and the total intensity field is the superposition $ I \( x_i \, y_m \) #h(0em) = #h(0em) sum_j #h(0em) sum_(k in cal(S)_j) Delta I_(i \, m)^(\( j \, k \)) . $ By construction, the deposition is energy-conserving on the mesh: $ sum_(i \, m) I \( x_i \, y_m \) thin Delta x thin Delta y thin tau #h(0em) = #h(0em) E_(upright(t r a n s)) \, $ to within machine precision, which we report as an energy check. The kernel-based deposition both preserves energy and anti-aliases sub-grid ray wandering, yielding smooth,