== Extraction of self-focusing observables
<app3.4>
From the reconstructed intensity map $I \( x \, y \)$ we determine the first self-focusing depth $z_(upright(s f))$ as the axial position of the global maximum along the optical axis (centerline $x = 0$): $ z_(upright(s f)) #h(0em) = #h(0em) "arg max"_y thin I \( 0 \, y \) . $ The lateral spot size at self-focus is measured by the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of the transverse profile at $y = z_(upright(s f))$. Defining $ I_perp \( x \) #h(0em) = #h(0em) I #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] x \, z_(upright(s f)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) I_max #h(0em) = #h(0em) I #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 0 \, z_(upright(s f)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $ the FWHM is the distance between the two half-maximum intersections, found by linear interpolation on the discrete grid: $ upright("FWHM") #h(0em) = #h(0em) x_2 - x_1 quad upright("where") quad I_perp \( x_1 \) #h(0em) = #h(0em) I_perp \( x_2 \) #h(0em) = #h(0em) 1 / 2 I_max . $ To quantify the portion of pulse energy participating directly in the self-focusing hotspot, we also report an integrated-energy metric within the "affected region," defined as the super-level set $ cal(A) #h(0em) = #h(0em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[{] \( x \, y \) #h(0em) : #h(0em) I \( x \, y \) gt.eq 0.05 thin I_max #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[}] . $ On the discrete mesh $\( x_i \, y_m \)$ with spacings $Delta x \, Delta y$ and pulse duration $tau$, the energy contained in this region and its fraction of the transmitted pulse are $ E_(cal(A)) #h(0em) = #h(0em) sum_(\( i \, m \) in cal(A)) I \( x_i \, y_m \) thin Delta x thin Delta y thin tau \, #h(2em) eta_(cal(A)) #h(0em) = #h(0em) E_(cal(A)) / E_(upright(t r a n s)) . $

== Numerical settings and domains
<app3.5>
All fields are computed on the rectangular domain $\[ - 25 \, 25 \] med mu upright(m) times \[ 0 \, 500 \] med mu upright(m)$ with a uniform Cartesian discretization of $N_x = 300$ and $N_y = 2000$ nodes. The geometric focus is prescribed at $z_f = 300 med mu upright(m)$. The Rayleigh range employs the waist diameter specified above, and spatial gradients of the nonlinear index $n \( x \, y \)$ are obtained by centered finite differences on the mesh and evaluated at off-grid ray locations by bilinear interpolation. The ray marching step is $d s = 0.5 med mu upright(m)$, which resolves the index-gradient curvature while maintaining numerical stability; halving $d s$ yields indistinguishable values for all reported metrics.

== Outputs used in this work
<app3.6>
For each simulation we save the reconstructed intensity map $I \( x \, y \)$, the Kerr-seeded Gaussian map $I_(upright(s e e d)) \( x \, y \)$, the nonlinear index $n \( x \, y \)$, and an energy-conservation diagnostic $ E_(upright(c h e c k)) = sum_(i \, m) I \( x_i \, y_m \) thin Delta x thin Delta y thin tau . $ We also record the first self-focusing depth $z_(upright(s f))$, the corresponding peak intensity $I_max$, the lateral full width at half maximum (FWHM) evaluated at $y = z_(upright(s f))$, and the energy contained in the affected region. All values quoted in the Results are computed directly from these fields produced by the algorithm described above.
]

#appendix("D", "app4", [Effect of spherical aberration-induced focal elongation])[
The potential influence of spherical aberration caused by the refractive index mismatch at the air/4H-SiC interface was quantitatively evaluated to assess whether it can account for the experimentally observed upstream shift of the modification depth.

For a high refractive index medium, the longitudinal focal shift and elongation can be estimated using geometrical optics relations \[#link(<ref-Sun05>)[33]\]. The corresponding longitudinal aberration length ($d_(upright(L A))$) can be expressed as $ d_(upright(L A)) = z / n_0 (sqrt(frac(n_0^2 - upright(N A)_(upright(e f f))^2, 1 - upright(N A)_(upright(e f f))^2)) - n_0) \, $ where $n_0$ is the refractive index of 4H-SiC and $upright(N A)_(upright(e f f))$ is the effective numerical aperture determined by the incident beam diameter.

Although the Mitutoyo objective lens has a nominal NA of 0.42, the entrance pupil diameter is 3.36 mm, whereas the incident beam diameter in our experiments was limited to 2 mm. Consequently, the effective numerical aperture is reduced to $upright(N A)_(upright(e f f))$ = 0.25. Using $n_0 = 2.55$ and a focal position of $z$ = 500 $mu$m below the 4H-SiC surface, the calculated longitudinal aberration length is $d_(upright(L A))$ = 13.9 $mu$m.

The result confirms that spherical aberration-induced focal elongation remains on the order of only a few micrometers under the present experimental conditions. Such a small elongation cannot account for the experimentally observed modification depth shifts on the order of hundreds of micrometers. Therefore, while spherical aberration contributes a static background broadening of the focal region, it cannot be the dominant mechanism governing the systematic upstream shift of the effective modification depth observed in this study.
]

#appendix("E", "app5", [Notation and definitions for modification depth analysis])[
At a single geometrical focus position on a modification track, suppose that $K$ principal modification points are identified along the depth direction. $K$ typically ranges from 2 to 5, depending on the pulse-overlap condition. We denote their depths by $z_(upright(m \, k))$ ($k = 1 \, dots.h \, K$). The centroid depth of the principal modification distribution within this single site is defined as $ z_(upright(m) \, p) = 1 / K sum_(k = 1)^K z_(upright(m) \, k) \, #h(2em) s_(upright(m) \, p) = upright(S D) (z_(upright(m) \, 1) \, dots.h \, z_(upright(m) \, K)) \, $ where $s_(m \, p)$ is the standard deviation (SD) of the $K$ principal points at the modification region. $z_(m \, p)$ is written without an overbar, which represents a within a track distribution.

For repeated measurements across $n$ tracks, we obtain a set of representative depths ${ z_(m \, p)^(\( i \)) }_(i = 1)^n$. The ensemble mean and its dispersion are defined as $ macron(z)_(upright(m) \, p) = 1 / n sum_(i = 1)^n z_(upright(m) \, p)^(\( i \)) \, #h(2em) s_(macron(z)_(upright(m) \, p)) = upright(S D) (z_(upright(m) \, p)^(\( 1 \)) \, dots.h \, z_(upright(m) \, p)^(\( n \))) \, $ where $macron(z)_(upright(m) \, p)$ denotes the ensemble-averaged representative depth and $s_(macron(z)_(upright(m) \, p))$ is the SD across the $n$ measurements.

Among the $K$ principal modification points identified at a single processing site, we define the primary modification depth, $z_(upright(m))$, as the incident side first modification point, $z_(upright(m)) equiv z_(upright(m \, 1))$, where the indexing is chosen such that $z_(upright(m \, 1))$ corresponds to the closest to the incident surface. For repeated cross-sectional tracks ($i = 1 \, dots.h \, n$), we shows the ensemble-averaged primary depth as $ macron(z)_(upright(m)) = 1 / n sum_(i = 1)^n z_(upright(m))^(\( i \)) \, #h(2em) s_(macron(z)_(upright(m))) = upright(S D) (z_(upright(m))^(\( 1 \)) \, dots.h \, z_(upright(m))^(\( n \))) \, $ where $s_(macron(z)_(upright(m)))$ denotes the standard deviation across the $n$ measurements.

In the following discussion, $z_(upright(m))$ (or $macron(z)_(upright(m))$ when averaged) is used as a key descriptor due to being typically most sensitive to the pulse energy-dependent onset of self-focusing-driven modification, whereas the emergence and spread of additional multifocal points are primarily governed by pulse overlap and the associated cumulative effects.
]

